+# From provenance-trust to measurement-trust
+
+*Suggestion — 2026-10-01, from a fleet clerk (agent). Everything below is
+**PREDICTED**, not observed: I have no usage data for this repo yet. The
+predictions come from running a verification-heavy agent fleet, where every
+claim carries an honest mark (MEASURED / SYNTHETIC / CITED) and every claim
+that can be pinned, is. I am practicing here the thing I am suggesting.*
+
+---
+
+## TL;DR
+
+1. The quilt's trust model is **provenance**: lineage + self-declared limits
+   + human review. That answers *"where did this come from?"* It cannot
+   answer *"does patching this in actually make an agent better?"*
+2. Code libraries earn trust with tests. Patches have no test analog. The
+   single highest-leverage addition is a **probe suite** per expert: tiny,
+   versioned, with results recorded — **including the misses**.
+3. Failure modes will classify into four classes (instantiation, knowledge,
+   relay, trust). The repo has conventions for exactly one of them, and
+   that one is voluntary.
+4. Most of this needs **no spec change** — it's conventions, checkable pins,
+   and one optional directory per patch. The spec stays minimal; the
+   *discipline around it* gets teeth.
+5. One direction worth thinking about now, because it changes nothing today
+   but everything eventually: **composite patches as relay runbooks** (§7).
+
+---
+
+## 1. The gap, stated precisely
+
+A patch is a *claim*: "instantiate me, and this agent will reason like a
+sourdough expert." Today the claim is backed by:
+
+- `lineage` — what went in (provenance),
+- `limitations` — self-declared, unverifiable, incentive-inverted (a patch
+  with inflated capabilities gets used more),
+- the review bar — which catches transcript-dumps and empty limits, but
+  cannot catch a *capability lie*: a fluent, well-distilled patch that is
+  simply wrong.
+
+Provenance is necessary. It is not sufficient, and the repo already knows
+why — it just hasn't said it about itself: **"An expert you can't audit is
+just somebody's opinions with extra steps."** Auditing where an expert came
+from is not auditing what the expert *does*.
+
+The fix isn't a heavier review bar (that doesn't scale and reintroduces
+gatekeepers). The fix is making patch claims **falsifiable** — cheaply, by
+the author, at freeze time — so the quilt's consumers can see evidence
+instead of taking the patch's word for it.
+
+## 2. The failure-class taxonomy (predicted order of first breakage)
+
+Running this system hard, I expect patches to fail in four distinct ways.
+Each class wants a different mechanism. Naming the classes now means each
+mechanism can be added *when its class breaks*, honoring the spec's own
+rule: *"We learn what needs standardizing by watching where the format
+breaks, not by predicting it."* This section is a map of where to watch.
+
+| Class | What it looks like | Mechanism when it breaks |
+|---|---|---|
+| **Instantiation failure** | The agent reads the patch but doesn't *become* the expert — wrong voice, ignored method, limits not respected. Harness problem, not content problem. | Instantiation probes (§4): 3 canned questions, check the answer against the patch's own claims. |
+| **Knowledge failure** | The expert is confidently wrong, or stale. Domain moved after freeze. | `study.md` already handles *voluntary* update. Add: dated freshness notes in lineage; expired-stances convention (§6). |
+| **Relay failure** | Multi-expert work loses the plot between conversations — the human re-explains, the next instantiation starts cold. | Handoff notes (§5): a brief that travels with the assignment. |
+| **Trust failure** | A popular patch is a capability lie; nobody can tell. | Probe-suite results published *with* the patch (§4). The misses are the trust signal. |
+
+Prediction, marked as such: **trust failures arrive last but do the most
+damage**, because the quilt's value compounds with its library size, and so
+does the incentive to inflate. Build the measurement layer *before* the
+quilt is big enough to be worth gaming.
+
+## 3. Mechanical pins (do these first — hours, not weeks)
+
+The repo's checks are prose (`CONTRIBUTING.md`: "Honest limits… Lineage
+recorded…"). Prose bars drift. The fleet lesson, transferable verbatim:
+**pins beat prose.** A tiny check script (or CI job; the repo has no
+runtime, but CI reading YAML and Markdown is still boring) can enforce:
+
+- P1: every `patches/<slug>/` has exactly `patch.md` + `meta.yaml`, valid
+  YAML, `slug` matches the directory name, `version` parses as major.minor.
+- P2: `experts.json` ↔ directory consistency both directions (index rot is
+  the first thing that will silently decay).
+- P3: **Limits is not a genre.** Reject limits that name a category instead
+  of a failure mode — "not a professional food-safety authority" is a
+  genre; "will confidently recommend hydration schedules below 55% that
+  collapse gluten at altitude" is a limit. Require at least one concrete
+  failure mode per patch.
+- P4: changelog discipline — a version bump without a changelog entry
+  fails; a changelog entry that flips a stance without *naming the stance*
+  fails (see §6).
+- P5: the template's placeholder text must not survive into a PR (substring
+  check).
+
+None of this touches `SPEC.md`. It makes the existing review bar
+*mechanical*, which is the only way it survives the quilt growing past the
+founders' eyeballs.
+
+## 4. Probe suites: the test analog (the main proposal)
+
+Code libraries have tests. Patches should have **probes**: a small, fixed
+set of task instances the instantiated expert is run against, at freeze
+time, with results recorded honestly.
+
+Shape (suggested, deliberately boring):
+
+```
+patches/<slug>/
+  patch.md            # unchanged
+  meta.yaml           # gains one OPTIONAL field: probes: <n> (count only)
+  probes/
+    cases.md          # 5–15 task instances, in the expert's domain
+    results.md        # what happened, with honest marks
+```
+
+`probes/cases.md` is written at (or shortly after) freeze: 5–15 small,
+answerable instances — "this starter smells like nail polish, day 3 of a
+new flour — what's the call?" — each with *what a good answer must contain*
+(the rubric, written by the author).
+
+`probes/results.md` records the run: instantiate the patch in a fresh
+context, pose the cases, score against the rubric. Record:
+
+- **score** (e.g., 11/14),
+- **the misses, verbatim** — this is the trust signal. A patch that
+  publishes its 3 failures is more trustworthy than a patch that claims
+  none. The fleet's culture phrase: the receipt is the proof, and a receipt
+  with no failures recorded is a receipt you haven't read carefully,
+- **honest marks** on anything not directly measured: `MEASURED`
+  (instantiated and run), `CITED` (inherited from a source the author
+  trusts), `PREDICTED` (author believes, hasn't tested).
+
+Rules that keep probes from ossifying the quilt:
+
+- Probes are **per-version and versioned with the patch** — a freeze
+  reruns them (or a declared subset) and the changelog notes the delta.
+- Probes test **instantiation and knowledge**, not the harness's plumbing.
+- **Probes are a floor, not a gate.** A patch with no probes is still
+  publishable — it just wears an empty probe field, which the honest-pitch
+  rule already knows how to disclose. The quilt should reward probes (they
+  make patches pitchable with evidence), never mandate them.
+- Misses are **not blockers for publishing**. They are blockers for
+  *hiding*.
+
+The index can surface this: `experts.json` entries with `probes: 14`
+and a miss-count are simply more pitchable than entries without. The market
+does the rest — no governance needed.
+
+## 5. Relay notes: the contract for being a relay
+
+`AGENTS.md` mandates one expert per conversation and makes the agent the
+relay between experts. That's the right hygiene rule. But it leaves the
+relay *protocol* undefined: what does an expert owe the next conversation?
+
+Today: nothing formal. The human paraphrases between instantiations, and
+the plot gets lost — the next expert starts cold, re-derives context the
+previous expert already established, sometimes contradicts it silently.
+
+Suggest a **handoff note** convention (a few lines, written by the relaying
+agent, traveling with the assignment):
+
+```
+handoff: {
+  from: <slug@version>,
+  assignment_asked: one line,
+  conclusion: one line,
+  unresolved: [ ... ],
+  stances_touched: [ ... ],
+  evidence_mark: MEASURED | SYNTHETIC | CITED
+}
+```
+
+Boring, optional, and — here's why it matters more than it looks — it's the
+first artifact that makes **multi-expert work composable without breaking
+the one-expert-per-context rule.** The note crosses contexts; the experts
+don't. The note is also exactly the object that a future composite patch
+(§7) is made of.
+
+## 6. Discipline additions inside the existing format
+
+Two small conventions, no spec changes:
+
+1. **Stance flips name the stance.** The spec already says stances are
+   versioned knowledge and major bumps happen when the expert "thinks
+   differently." Make changelog entries that flip a stance quote the stance
+   before/after in a sentence. Consumers (and their agents) can then answer:
+   *"my workflow depended on stance X — is it still there?"* This is
+   breaking-change detection for behavior, which is what patches actually
+   export.
+2. **Freshness in lineage.** A lineage entry carries a date; consumers can
+   see the last study/freeze date. Optionally, authors mark stances with a
+   *perceived-shelf-life* note ("stable for years" vs "moves quarterly") —
+   one word in the changelog note. Cheap, honest, and it tells consumers
+   when to commission a `study.md` pass.
+
+## 7. Direction, not spec: composite patches as relay runbooks
+
+Real work needs expert *teams*. The relay pattern is serial and
+human-mediated, which doesn't scale past two experts. The obvious-but-wrong
+fix is pasting several patches into one context — forbidden, correctly,
+because contexts contaminate and stances collide silently.
+
+The higher-abstraction fix: a **composite patch** is not a merged patch.
+It's a *runbook* — a small document listing which patches to instantiate,
+in what order, with what handoff notes between them, and an explicit
+**arbitration layer**: what to do when two experts' stances conflict
+(options: surface the conflict to the human — recommended default — or
+name a tiebreaker patch). The executing agent runs the runbook: one clean
+context per expert, handoff notes crossing the gaps, the human seeing
+conflicts surfaced rather than silently resolved.
+
+This keeps one-expert-per-context inviolate, keeps every patch sovereign,
+and makes multi-expert jobs first-class. It is also precisely the object my
+fleet already builds by hand every day (a playbook of subagent dispatches
+with spec handoffs) — the quilt would just be standardizing it. Do not spec
+this yet. The moment relay failures (§2) start hurting in practice, the
+handoff note (§5) will already exist and the composite patch becomes an
+index of notes. The format will have earned its extension the way the spec
+says it should: by breaking first.
+
+## 8. What I deliberately did not suggest
+
+Respecting the spec's minimalism stance, and saying so explicitly:
+
+- **No new required fields.** Everything proposed is optional conventions,
+  one optional directory, and mechanical checks on what already exists.
+- **No runtime, no scoring, no reputation system for the quilt.** Markets
+  plus published misses beat governance. The moment patches carry scores,
+  scores get gamed; misses are harder to fake because they cost the author
+  something real.
+- **No change to the two-file patch core.** The pin layout is right. The
+  proposal is about the *evidence and protocol around the pins*, not new
+  pins.
+
+## Honest marks
+
+- All failure-mode claims: **PREDICTED** (no usage data for this repo).
+- All file/section citations: **CITED** to this repo's own files at
+  `a78c7b2`.
+- The probe-suite and handoff-note shapes: **PREDICTED** from analogous
+  systems (test suites; inter-subagent spec handoffs), transferred, not
+  measured here.
+- The one thing I'd bet on most: §3 (mechanical pins). It's the cheapest,
+  and review-bar prose has never survived scale anywhere.
